(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["rhetorical-synthesis_2"] = [{"id":"92dec236","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Maika’i Tubbs is a Native Hawaiian sculptor and installation artist. His work has been shown in the United States, Canada, Japan, and Germany, among other places. Many of his sculptures feature discarded objects. His work Erasure (2008) includes discarded audiocassette tapes and magnets. His work Home Grown (2009) includes discarded pushpins, plastic plates and forks, and wood. The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two works.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Maika’i Tubbs is a Native Hawaiian sculptor and installation artist.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"His work has been shown in the United States, Canada, Japan, and Germany, among other places.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Many of his sculptures feature discarded objects.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"His work Erasure (2008) includes discarded audiocassette tapes and magnets.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"His work Home Grown (2009) includes discarded pushpins, plastic plates and forks, and wood.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Erasure (2008) uses discarded objects such as audiocassette tapes and magnets; Home Grown (2009), however, includes pushpins, plastic plates and forks, and wood.","Tubbs’s work, which often features discarded objects, has been shown both within the United States and abroad.","Like many of Tubbs’s sculptures, both Erasure and Home Grown include discarded objects: Erasure uses audiocassette tapes, and Home Grown uses plastic forks.","Tubbs completed Erasure in 2008 and Home Grown in 2009."]},{"id":"5b8b69a2","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Archaeologist Jon Erlandson and colleagues argue that humans first arrived in the Americas by sea. They propose that humans traveled between Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines from northeast Asia to the Americas. Many of these islands and coastal zones were later submerged as glaciers melted and sea levels rose. The researchers think that “a coastal route, including kelp forests and estuaries, would have provided a rich mix of marine, estuarine, riverine, and terrestrial resources” such as seaweeds, fish, and birds. This proposed scenario is known as the kelp highway hypothesis. The student wants to summarize the kelp highway hypothesis.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Archaeologist Jon Erlandson and colleagues argue that humans first arrived in the Americas by sea.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"They propose that humans traveled between Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines from northeast Asia to the Americas.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Many of these islands and coastal zones were later submerged as glaciers melted and sea levels rose.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The researchers think that “a coastal route, including kelp forests and estuaries, would have provided a rich mix of marine, estuarine,","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"riverine, and terrestrial resources” such as seaweeds, fish, and birds.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"This proposed scenario is known as the kelp highway hypothesis.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines likely contained “a rich mix of marine, estuarine, riverine, and terrestrial resources” such as seaweeds, fish, and birds, according to researchers.","One argument about how humans first arrived in the Americas is the kelp highway hypothesis proposed by Jon Erlandson and colleagues.","Humans may have first arrived in the Americas by sea, traveling between Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines and subsisting on a variety of resources.","As glaciers melted and sea levels rose, many Pacific Ocean islands and coastal zones were submerged."]},{"id":"81315093","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: A marathon is a long-distance running race that is 26.2 miles long. An ultramarathon is a long-distance running race of more than 26.2 miles. The Kepler Challenge is a one-day, 37.3-mile ultramarathon in New Zealand. The Spreelauf is a six-day, 261-mile ultramarathon in Germany. The student wants to make a generalization about ultramarathons.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"A marathon is a long-distance running race that is 26.2 miles long.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"An ultramarathon is a long-distance running race of more than 26.2 miles.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The Kepler Challenge is a one-day, 37.3-mile ultramarathon in New Zealand.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The Spreelauf is a six-day, 261-mile ultramarathon in Germany.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Examples of ultramarathons include the 37.3-mile Kepler Challenge in New Zealand and the 261-mile Spreelauf in Germany.","A marathon is 26.2 miles long, but the Spreelauf ultramarathon, at 261 miles, is far longer.","Ultramarathons range widely in length, from a few dozen miles to a few hundred.","While the Kepler Challenge is a one-day ultramarathon, the Spreelauf is a six-day ultramarathon."]},{"id":"973632d2","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: In North America, woodlands have expanded into areas that were once grasslands. Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell of Wichita State University investigated whether woodland expansion is related to changes in climate. Rogers and Russell analyzed core samples from oak trees on a site that was not wooded in the past and indexed the age of the trees with historical climate data to see if tree populations and climate were correlated. Tree population growth was associated with dry intervals. Droughts may have played a role in woodland expansion. The student wants to emphasize the aim of the research study.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"In North America, woodlands have expanded into areas that were once grasslands.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell of Wichita State University investigated whether woodland expansion is related to changes in","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"climate.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Rogers and Russell analyzed core samples from oak trees on a site that was not wooded in the past and indexed the age of the trees with","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"historical climate data to see if tree populations and climate were correlated.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Tree population growth was associated with dry intervals.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Droughts may have played a role in woodland expansion.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell, researchers at Wichita State University, wanted to know if woodland expansion is related to changes in climate.","Thanks to the work done by Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell, we now know that droughts may have played a role in woodland expansion.","Wichita State University researchers have determined that tree population growth was associated with dry intervals.","Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell analyzed core samples from oak trees on a site that was not wooded in the past, indexing the age of the trees with historical climate data."]},{"id":"99183985","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Some sandstone arches in Utah’s Arches National Park have been defaced by tourists’ carvings. Park rangers can smooth away some carvings using power grinders. For deep carvings, power grinding is not always feasible because it can greatly alter or damage the rock. Park rangers can use an infilling technique, which involves filling in carvings with ground sandstone and a bonding agent. This technique is minimally invasive. The student wants to explain an advantage of the infilling technique.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Some sandstone arches in Utah’s Arches National Park have been defaced by tourists’ carvings.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Park rangers can smooth away some carvings using power grinders.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"For deep carvings, power grinding is not always feasible because it can greatly alter or damage the rock.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Park rangers can use an infilling technique, which involves filling in carvings with ground sandstone and a bonding agent.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"This technique is minimally invasive.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To remove carvings from sandstone arches in Utah’s Arches National Park, power grinding is not always feasible.","Filling in carvings with ground sandstone and a bonding agent is less invasive than smoothing them away with a power grinder, which can greatly alter or damage the sandstone arches.","Park rangers can use a power grinding technique to smooth away carvings or fill them in with ground sandstone and a bonding agent.","As methods for removing carvings from sandstone, power grinding and infilling differ in their level of invasiveness."]},{"id":"7fa2b1ee","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: In meteorology, an air mass is a large body of air with generally uniform humidity and temperature. Air masses are commonly classified by two-letter names. The first letter indicates the humidity of the air mass, while the second letter indicates the temperature. cA (continental arctic) means dry and cold, for example. mT (maritime tropical) means moist and warm. This classification system is based on the work of a Swedish meteorologist named Tor Bergeron (1891 – 1977). The student wants to provide an example of an air mass.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"In meteorology, an air mass is a large body of air with generally uniform humidity and temperature.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Air masses are commonly classified by two-letter names.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The first letter indicates the humidity of the air mass, while the second letter indicates the temperature.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"cA (continental arctic) means dry and cold, for example.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"mT (maritime tropical) means moist and warm.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"This classification system is based on the work of a Swedish meteorologist named Tor Bergeron (1891–1977).","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Air masses are large bodies of air with generally uniform humidity and temperature.","The air mass classification system uses two-letter names and is based on the work of Tor Bergeron, a Swedish meteorologist.","Air masses are commonly classified by a two-letter name that indicates humidity and temperature.","One type of air mass is known as a cA, or continental arctic, air mass because it is dry and cold."]},{"id":"ce282575","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: J.R.R. Tolkien’s 1937 novel The Hobbit features two maps. The novel opens with a reproduction of the map that the characters use on their quest. This map introduces readers to the fictional world they are about to enter. The novel closes with a map depicting every stop on the characters’ journey. That map allows readers to reconstruct the story they have just read. The student wants to contrast the purposes of the two maps in The Hobbit.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"J.R.R. Tolkien’s 1937 novel The Hobbit features two maps.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The novel opens with a reproduction of the map that the characters use on their quest.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"This map introduces readers to the fictional world they are about to enter.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The novel closes with a map depicting every stop on the characters’ journey.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"That map allows readers to reconstruct the story they have just read.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Hobbit’s opening map introduces readers to the fictional world they are about to enter, while the closing map allows them to reconstruct the story they have just read.","The Hobbit, a novel published by J.R.R. Tolkien in 1937, features a reproduction of a map that the characters use on their quest, as well as a map that appears at the end of the novel.","The Hobbit’s two maps, one opening and one closing the novel, each serve a purpose for readers.","In 1937, author J.R.R. Tolkien published The Hobbit, a novel featuring both an opening and a closing map."]},{"id":"63a4fa29","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: In 2013, archaeologists studied cat bone fragments they had found in the ruins of Quanhucun, a Chinese farming village. The fragments were estimated to be 5,300 years old. A chemical analysis of the fragments revealed that the cats had consumed large amounts of grain. The grain consumption is evidence that the Quanhucun cats may have been domesticated. The student wants to present the Quanhucun study and its conclusions.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"In 2013, archaeologists studied cat bone fragments they had found in the ruins of Quanhucun, a Chinese farming village.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The fragments were estimated to be 5,300 years old.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"A chemical analysis of the fragments revealed that the cats had consumed large amounts of grain.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The grain consumption is evidence that the Quanhucun cats may have been domesticated.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["As part of a 2013 study of cat domestication, a chemical analysis was conducted on cat bone fragments found in Quanhucun, China.","A 2013 analysis of cat bone fragments found in Quanhucun, China, suggests that cats there may have been domesticated 5,300 years ago.","In 2013, archaeologists studied what cats in Quanhucun, China, had eaten more than 5,000 years ago.","Cat bone fragments estimated to be 5,300 years old were found in Quanhucun, China, in 2013."]},{"id":"bb275f0d","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Cities tend to have a wide range of flowering vegetation in parks, yards, and gardens. This vegetation provides a varied diet for honeybees, strengthening bees’ immune systems. On average, 62.5 percent of bees in an urban area will survive a harsh winter. Rural areas are often dominated by monoculture crops such as corn or wheat. On average, only 40 percent of honeybees in a rural area will survive a harsh winter. The student wants to make and support a generalization about honeybees.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Cities tend to have a wide range of flowering vegetation in parks, yards, and gardens.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"This vegetation provides a varied diet for honeybees, strengthening bees’ immune systems.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"On average, 62.5 percent of bees in an urban area will survive a harsh winter.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Rural areas are often dominated by monoculture crops such as corn or wheat.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"On average, only 40 percent of honeybees in a rural area will survive a harsh winter.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Cities tend to have a wider range of flowering vegetation than do rural areas, which are often dominated by monoculture crops.","In urban areas, over 60 percent of honeybees, on average, will survive a harsh winter, whereas in rural areas, only 40 percent will.","The strength of honeybees’ immune systems depends on what the bees eat, and a varied diet is more available to bees in an urban area than to those in a rural area.","Honeybees are more likely to thrive in cities than in rural areas because the varied diet available in urban areas strengthens the bees’ immune systems."]},{"id":"39ccb463","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: The Atlantic Monthly magazine was first published in 1857. The magazine focused on politics, art, and literature. In 2019, historian Cathryn Halverson published the book Faraway Women and the “Atlantic Monthly.” Its subject is female authors whose autobiographies appeared in the magazine in the early 1900s. One of the authors discussed is Juanita Harrison. The student wants to introduce Cathryn Halverson’s book to an audience already familiar with the Atlantic Monthly.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The Atlantic Monthly magazine was first published in 1857.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The magazine focused on politics, art, and literature.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"In 2019, historian Cathryn Halverson published the book Faraway Women and the “Atlantic Monthly.”","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Its subject is female authors whose autobiographies appeared in the magazine in the early 1900s.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"One of the authors discussed is Juanita Harrison.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Cathryn Halverson’s Faraway Women and the “Atlantic Monthly” discusses female authors whose autobiographies appeared in the magazine in the early 1900s.","A magazine called the Atlantic Monthly, referred to in Cathryn Halverson’s book title, was first published in 1857.","Faraway Women and the “Atlantic Monthly” features contributors to the Atlantic Monthly, first published in 1857 as a magazine focusing on politics, art, and literature.","An author discussed by Cathryn Halverson is Juanita Harrison, whose autobiography appeared in the Atlantic Monthly in the early 1900s."]},{"id":"e2693197","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Oracles of the Pink Universe was a 2021 exhibition at the Denver Museum of Art in Colorado. It featured eight artworks by South African artist Simphiwe Ndzube. One of these works is a painting titled Assertion of Will. Assertion of Will depicts three standing figures. The figures wear clothing made of fabric pieces stitched to the painting’s canvas. The student wants to describe how fabric is used in Assertion of Will.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Oracles of the Pink Universe was a 2021 exhibition at the Denver Museum of Art in Colorado.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"It featured eight artworks by South African artist Simphiwe Ndzube.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"One of these works is a painting titled Assertion of Will.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Assertion of Will depicts three standing figures.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The figures wear clothing made of fabric pieces stitched to the painting’s canvas.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["In Assertion of Will, the figures’ clothing is made of fabric pieces stitched to the painting’s canvas.","The exhibition Oracles of the Pink Universe featured artworks by artist Simphiwe Ndzube.","Depicting three standing, clothed figures, Assertion of Will is a painting by Simphiwe Ndzube.","Simphiwe Ndzube’s Assertion of Will was one of eight artworks exhibited in Oracles of the Pink Universe at the Denver Museum of Art."]},{"id":"70538b9a","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: In the 1930s, the Imperial Sugar Cane Institute in India sought to limit the country’s dependence on imported sugarcane. The institute enlisted botanist Janaki Ammal to breed a local variety of sugarcane. She crossbred the imported sugarcane species Saccharum officinarum with grasses native to India. She succeeded in creating sugarcane hybrids well suited to India’s climate. The student wants to emphasize Janaki Ammal’s achievement.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"In the 1930s, the Imperial Sugar Cane Institute in India sought to limit the country’s dependence on imported sugarcane.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The institute enlisted botanist Janaki Ammal to breed a local variety of sugarcane.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"She crossbred the imported sugarcane species Saccharum officinarum with grasses native to India.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"She succeeded in creating sugarcane hybrids well suited to India’s climate.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["By crossbreeding the imported sugarcane species Saccharum officinarum with grasses native to India, Ammal succeeded in creating sugarcane hybrids well suited to India’s climate.","In the 1930s, the Imperial Sugar Cane Institute, which enlisted Ammal, sought to limit dependence on imported sugarcane.","Ammal was enlisted by the Imperial Sugar Cane Institute at a time when a local variety of sugarcane needed to be produced.","As part of efforts to breed a local variety of sugarcane, an imported sugarcane species called Saccharum officinarum was crossbred with grasses native to India."]},{"id":"5222ffab","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Neuroscientists Krishnan Padmanabhan and Zhen Chen sought to better understand the workings of the brain’s olfactory system. They devised a study using mathematical models. They found that certain fibers allow the brain to toggle from one method of processing smells to another. In one method, cells in the piriform cortex (where the perception of odor forms) capture olfactory information at a given moment. In the other, the cells track changes in olfactory information over time. The student wants to summarize the study’s findings.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Neuroscientists Krishnan Padmanabhan and Zhen Chen sought to better understand the workings of the brain’s olfactory system.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"They devised a study using mathematical models.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"They found that certain fibers allow the brain to toggle from one method of processing smells to another.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"In one method, cells in the piriform cortex (where the perception of odor forms) capture olfactory information at a given moment.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"In the other, the cells track changes in olfactory information over time.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To arrive at these findings, which describe dual methods of processing smells in the piriform cortex, Padmanabhan and Chen devised a study using mathematical models.","Padmanabhan and Chen showed that olfactory information is captured by cells in the piriform cortex, where the perception of odor forms.","Using mathematical models, Padmanabhan and Chen devised a study to better understand the workings of the brain’s olfactory system.","According to Padmanabhan and Chen, the brain can toggle between capturing olfactory information at a given moment and tracking changes in that information over time."]},{"id":"56b000d0","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: The factors that affect clutch size (the number of eggs laid at one time) have been well studied in birds but not in lizards. A team led by Shai Meiri of Tel Aviv University investigated which factors influence lizard clutch size. Meiri’s team obtained clutch-size and habitat data for over 3,900 lizard species and analyzed the data with statistical models. Larger clutch size was associated with environments in higher latitudes that have more seasonal change. Lizards in higher-latitude environments may lay larger clutches to take advantage of shorter windows of favorable conditions. The student wants to emphasize the aim of the research study.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The factors that affect clutch size (the number of eggs laid at one time) have been well studied in birds but not in lizards.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"A team led by Shai Meiri of Tel Aviv University investigated which factors influence lizard clutch size.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Meiri’s team obtained clutch-size and habitat data for over 3,900 lizard species and analyzed the data with statistical models.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Larger clutch size was associated with environments in higher latitudes that have more seasonal change.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Lizards in higher-latitude environments may lay larger clutches to take advantage of shorter windows of favorable conditions.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Researchers wanted to know which factors influence lizard egg clutch size because such factors have been well studied in birds but not in lizards.","After they obtained data for over 3,900 lizard species, researchers determined that larger clutch size was associated with environments in higher latitudes that have more seasonal change.","We now know that lizards in higher-latitude environments may lay larger clutches to take advantage of shorter windows of favorable conditions.","Researchers obtained clutch-size and habitat data for over 3,900 lizard species and analyzed the data with statistical models."]},{"id":"17ec916d","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Bharati Mukherjee was an Indian-born author of novels and short stories. She published the novel The Holder of the World in 1993. A central character in the novel is a woman living in twentieth-century United States. Another central character is a woman living in seventeenth-century India. The student wants to introduce the novel The Holder of the World to an audience already familiar with Bharati Mukherjee.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Bharati Mukherjee was an Indian-born author of novels and short stories.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"She published the novel The Holder of the World in 1993.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"A central character in the novel is a woman living in twentieth-century United States.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Another central character is a woman living in seventeenth-century India.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Bharati Mukherjee’s settings include both twentieth-century United States and seventeenth-century India.","In addition to her novel The Holder of the World, which was published in 1993, Indian-born author Bharati Mukherjee wrote other novels and short stories.","Bharati Mukherjee’s novel The Holder of the World centers around two women, one living in twentieth-century United States and the other in seventeenth-century India.","The Holder of the World was not the only novel written by Indian-born author Bharati Mukherjee."]}];
